%I A395423 #35 Jul 29 2026 16:45:24
%S A395423 3,6,17,116
%N A395423 Number of dimensions of the Poisson-bracket Lie algebra generated by the pairwise interaction Hamiltonians of a 3-body system with a singular pairwise potential.
%C A395423 Setting. Three point particles move in the plane. Particle i has position (x_i, y_i) and conjugate momentum (px_i, py_i), for i = 1, 2, 3; the phase space is the resulting 12-dimensional space. Let r_{ij} = sqrt((x_i - x_j)^2 + (y_i - y_j)^2) be the distance between particles i and j, and let V be a fixed real function (the pairwise potential). The energy of pair (i, j) is the function H_{ij} = (px_i^2 + py_i^2)/2 + (px_j^2 + py_j^2)/2 + V(r_{ij}).
%C A395423 Poisson bracket. For two smooth functions f, g of the positions and momenta, their canonical Poisson bracket is {f, g} = Sum_{i=1..3} ( (df/dx_i)(dg/dpx_i) - (df/dpx_i)(dg/dx_i) + (df/dy_i)(dg/dpy_i) - (df/dpy_i)(dg/dy_i) ). This operation is antisymmetric and satisfies the Jacobi identity, so the smooth functions form a Lie algebra under it.
%C A395423 Construction. Set L_0 = span{H_{12}, H_{13}, H_{23}}, the real vector space spanned by the three pairwise energies. For n >= 0 set L_{n+1} = L_n + {L_n, L_n}, where {L_n, L_n} is the linear span of all brackets {f, g} with f, g in L_n. Then a(n) = dim L_n. The sequence is nondecreasing by construction.
%C A395423 General interest. The dimension sequence 3, 6, 17, 116 is empirically universal: it is the same for every singular pairwise potential tested. This has been verified for V(r) = 1/r (Newtonian gravity), 1/r^2 (the Calogero-Moser model), 1/r^3, log(r), the Yukawa potential exp(-mu*r)/r, and several composite potentials Sum_k c_k/r^{p_k}; in spatial dimension d = 1, 2, and 3; and for arbitrary unequal positive masses (with masses, the pair energy is H_{ij} = (px_i^2 + py_i^2)/(2*m_i) + (px_j^2 + py_j^2)/(2*m_j) + m_i*m_j*V(r_{ij})). The four values were reproduced independently in three computer algebra systems (sympy, Mathematica, SageMath).
%C A395423 Replacing V by the harmonic potential V(r) = r^2 breaks the universality: the analogous sequence is 3, 6, 13, 15, 15, 15, ..., closing at dimension 15. So the universal growth is not shared by the harmonic potential.
%C A395423 a(3) = 116 was verified two independent ways: (i) by exact rank of the generators' coefficient matrix over the rational numbers, computed independently in sympy (the program below) and in Mathematica 14.3 (sparse exact rational rank, about 40 seconds on one core); and (ii) numerically, by evaluating the symbolic generators on a phase-space grid and taking the singular-value-decomposition rank, where the gap between singular values 116 and 117 exceeds 10^9, cleanly separating rank 116 from numerical noise. All methods agree.
%D A395423 V. I. Arnold, Mathematical Methods of Classical Mechanics, 2nd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics 60, Springer, 1989. (Poisson bracket: section 40; symplectic phase space: chapter 8.)
%H A395423 Brian J. Sheppard, <a href="https://github.com/bshepp/3body-poisson-algebra">3body-poisson-algebra</a> (GitHub repository with reproduction scripts).
%H A395423 Brian J. Sheppard, <a href="/A395423/a395423.txt">Python program (self-contained, requires sympy >= 1.13.3)</a>.
%H A395423 Brian J. Sheppard, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18899803">Super-exponential growth of the Poisson algebra in the planar three-body problem</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%e A395423 a(0) = 3: the three pairwise Hamiltonians H_{12}, H_{13}, H_{23} are linearly independent functions, so dim L_0 = 3.
%e A395423 a(1) = 6: the three brackets {H_{12}, H_{13}}, {H_{12}, H_{23}}, {H_{13}, H_{23}} are linearly independent of one another and of L_0, adding 3 new generators, so dim L_1 = 6. For example, with V(r) = 1/r, {H_{12}, H_{13}} = (px_1*(x_1 - x_3) + py_1*(y_1 - y_3))/r_{13}^3 - (px_1*(x_1 - x_2) + py_1*(y_1 - y_2))/r_{12}^3, a function on phase space independent of L_0.
%e A395423 a(2) = 17: among the binomial(6,2) = 15 brackets of pairs from L_1, eleven add new independent generators, so dim L_2 = 17.
%e A395423 a(3) = 116: among the binomial(17,2) = 136 brackets of pairs from L_2, ninety-nine add new independent generators, so dim L_3 = 116.
